<time datetime="2023-03-13">March 13, 2023</time>
<article id="Email" class="WebBits dictionaryWidget">
    <h2>GUIDE: Electronic Mail</h2>
    <p>
        E-mail is not a protocol.
        <abbr title="Simple Mail Transfer Protocol">SMTP</abbr> is a protocol.
        E-mail, and the protocols in use with e-mail sending/receiving, is
        complicated to integrate into a website natively. It will be easier to
        first learn by knowing the basics.
    </p>

    <p>
        A general discussion will reveal there are many ports operating email.
        The below definitions help to explain some of the mechanics underlying
        the definitions.
    </p>
    <aside class="exampleBlue">
        <ul>
            <li><span class="client">Green:</span> Default port</li>
            <li>
                <span class="clientconf">Yellow:</span> May require configuration
                &lpar;i.e. certificates &rpar;
            </li>
            <li>
                <span class="server">Red:</span> Server ports. Not normally
                available to client PCs.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </aside>
    <ul>
        <li><strong class="client">Port 25</strong> SMTP</li>
        <li><strong class="client">Port 109</strong> POP2</li>
        <li><strong class="client">Port 110</strong> POP3</li>
        <li><strong class="client">Port 143</strong> IMAP4</li>
        <li><strong class="server">Port 465</strong> SMTPS</li>
        <li>
            <strong class="server">Port 587</strong> MSA &lpar;used by SMTP
            servers&rpar;
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong class="clientconf">Port 993</strong> IMAP4 (encrypted)
        </li>
        <li><strong class="clientconf">Port 995</strong> POP3 (encrypted)</li>
        <li>Other: <strong class="clientconf">2525, 32587, etc.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <br>
    <figure class="imgShadow">
        <img class="imgPTR" src="../library/img/SMTP_commands_overview.png" alt="SMTP client-to-server messages">
        <figcaption>
            <strong>Source:</strong>
            <a href="https://postmarkapp.com/guides/everything-you-need-to-know-about-smtp" target="_blank" rel="external noopener">
                Postmark,
                Everything you need to know about SMTP &lpar;Simple
                Mail Transfer Protocol&rpar;
            </a>
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl>
        <dt>Internet Message Acces Protocol &lpar;IMAP&rpar;</dt>
        <dd>
            Supports connecting multiple clients to the same mailbox
            simultaneously.
        </dd>
        <dt>Mail Submission Agents &lpar;MSA&rpar;</dt>
        <dd>
            Used by mail clients to submit messages for delivery by an SMTP
            server.
        </dd>
        <dt>Post-Office Protocol &lpar;POP&rpar;</dt>
        <dd>
            Stores messages delivered by SMTP. <i>a.k.a.</i> POP3 downloads the
            messages to recipient client.
        </dd>
        <dt>Simple Mail Transfer Protocol &lpar;SMTP&rpar;</dt>
        <dd>
            Used to transport mail for message relay. STARTTLS can be enabled.
        </dd>
        <dt>Simple Mail Transfer Protocol Secure&lpar;SMTPS&rpar;</dt>
        <dd>SMTP encrypted by implicit TLS.</dd>
    </dl>
</article>
